">synthetic-140</div><div class=\"what\">Word: a list typed with bullet characters instead of list formatting</div><span class=\"chip held\">CAUGHT</span></div><div class=\"trapm\"><div class=\"name\">synthetic-141</div><div class=\"what\">Word: the same list built with Word's numbering</div><span class=\"chip clean\">PASSED CLEAN</span></div><div class=\"trapm\"><div class=\"name\">synthetic-142</div><div class=\"what\">Word: links reading “click here” and “read more”</div><span class=\"chip clean\">PASSED CLEAN</span></div><div class=\"trapm\"><div class=\"name\">synthetic-143</div><div class=\"what\">Word: a link with no link text at all</div><span class=\"chip held\">CAUGHT</span></div><div class=\"trapm\"><div class=\"name\">synthetic-144</div><div class=\"what\">Word: the same page with both links described</div><span class=\"chip clean\">PASSED CLEAN</span></div><div class=\"trapm\"><div class=\"name\">synthetic-145</div><div class=\"what\">PowerPoint: headings typed into text boxes instead of title placeholders</div><span class=\"chip held\">CAUGHT</span></div><div class=\"trapm\"><div class=\"name\">synthetic-146</div><div class=\"what\">PowerPoint: the same slides with real title placeholders</div><span class=\"chip clean\">PASSED CLEAN</span></div><div class=\"trapm\"><div class=\"name\">synthetic-147</div><div class=\"what\">PowerPoint: a slide with no heading at all — AAA, and not scored</div><span class=\"chip clean\">PASSED CLEAN</span></div><div class=\"trapm\"><div class=\"name\">synthetic-148</div><div class=\"what\">PowerPoint: a big statistic in a body placeholder — content, not a heading</div><span class=\"chip clean\">PASSED CLEAN</span></div><div class=\"trapm\"><div class=\"name\">synthetic-149</div><div class=\"what\">PowerPoint: a long sentence set large — emphasis, not a heading</div><span class=\"chip clean\">PASSED CLEAN</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div id=\"all-tests\" class=\"trapmodal\" role=\"dialog\" aria-label=\"How this app is tested before anything ships\">\n    <div class=\"tm-inner\">\n      <div class=\"tm-head\">\n        <div>\n          <div class=\"tm-title\">How this app is tested before anything ships</div>\n          <div class=\"tm-sub\">Twelve gates stand between a code change and the live site &mdash; run automatically, in this order of ambition, on every single change. One failure stops the release cold. These are the ones that exist to guarantee the ACCURACY of your report, not just that the buttons work.</div>\n        </div>\n        <a class=\"tm-close\" href=\"#top\">&#10005; Close &mdash; back to the page</a>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"tm-grid\">\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">every change</div><div class=\"what\">3,437 self-checks &mdash; every promise the software has ever made, re-tested from the top</div><span class=\"chip clean\">EVERY CHANGE</span></div>\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">every change</div><div class=\"what\">The 149 trap documents &mdash; rebuilt from scratch and re-judged; every designed answer must come back right</div><span class=\"chip clean\">EVERY CHANGE</span></div>\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">every change</div><div class=\"what\">The scoring invariant &mdash; the grade shown must always match the score computed; the one test that may never be removed</div><span class=\"chip clean\">EVERY CHANGE</span></div>\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">every change</div><div class=\"what\">The golden score ledger &mdash; every control document&rsquo;s exact score and per-category verdict is pinned; no grade can move unless a person approves the movement in review</div><span class=\"chip clean\">EVERY CHANGE</span></div>\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">every change</div><div class=\"what\">The re-save test &mdash; every trap rewritten by a different tool must grade identically, digit for digit; byte layout can never change a grade</div><span class=\"chip clean\">EVERY CHANGE</span></div>\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">every change</div><div class=\"what\">Same file, same answer &mdash; sentinel documents audited five times, three of them at once: identical results, every time, even under load</div><span class=\"chip clean\">EVERY CHANGE</span></div>\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">every change</div><div class=\"what\">Real-pipeline integration &mdash; the actual tools on actual PDFs, end to end; no simulations standing in for the real thing</div><span class=\"chip clean\">EVERY CHANGE</span></div>\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">every change</div><div class=\"what\">The wiring checks &mdash; pages must render what the data claims: the trap list must hold exactly as many documents as the page says, and this page must match the emailable version word for word</div><span class=\"chip clean\">EVERY CHANGE</span></div>\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">every change</div><div class=\"what\">Type, lint, and build guards &mdash; the unglamorous gates that stop whole classes of mistakes before they are even runnable</div><span class=\"chip clean\">EVERY CHANGE</span></div>\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">before release</div><div class=\"what\">The full corpus sweep &mdash; every real agency document alongside every trap, with the same invariants verified on each: no category may lose points without naming the WCAG rule it broke, and nothing the law requires may be filed under &ldquo;not scored&rdquo;</div><span class=\"chip rel\">BEFORE RELEASE</span></div>\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">after deploy</div><div class=\"what\">Live-site sentinels &mdash; known traps uploaded to the real site after a deploy: the scan lie must still score 0, the perfect document must still score 100</div><span class=\"chip held\">AFTER DEPLOY</span></div>\n        <div class=\"trapm testm\"><div class=\"name\">every report</div><div class=\"what\">The independent referee &mdash; veraPDF runs beside the checker on every single report in production; a second opinion that never sleeps</div><span class=\"chip held\">EVERY REPORT</span></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  </section>\n\n  <section>\n    <div class=\"kicker\">the feedback loop &middot; internal &amp; external specialists</div>\n    <h2 class=\"display\">Honed with internal and external accessibility specialists.</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">This is not an internal experiment. The checker is <strong>used every day &mdash; by ICJIA and by outside agencies and document specialists</strong> &mdash; and tuned in working sessions with accessibility specialists inside and outside the agency: real documents, real disputes, fixes shipped the same day. Four grade challenges from document experts and state reviewers, each settled by reading the file itself &mdash; never by defending the tool. Don't take the \"every day\" on faith either &mdash; <a href=\"https://audit.icjia.app/status?html\" style=\"color:var(--act);font-weight:600\">the public stats page</a> counts every check-up as it happens.</p>\n    <div class=\"disputes\">\n      <div class=\"dis\"><div class=\"who\">State accessibility team &middot; a form</div>\n        <div class=\"grade\"><span class=\"from\">65 D</span> <span class=\"arrow\">&rarr;</span> <span class=\"to\">100 A</span></div>\n        <p>The expert was right. The checker mis-measured forms. Fixed and published the same day.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"dis\"><div class=\"who\">A document-repair specialist &middot; two \"missing\" images</div>\n        <div class=\"grade\"><span class=\"from\">79 C</span> <span class=\"arrow\">&rarr;</span> <span class=\"to\">100 A</span></div>\n        <p>Right again &mdash; the \"images\" were leftovers from a rebuilt page, on no page at all. Fixed the same day.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"dis\"><div class=\"who\">Another expert &middot; table headers</div>\n        <div class=\"grade\"><span class=\"right\">Checker was right &#10003;</span></div>\n        <p>Verified against the file's internals &mdash; the flag was real. The advice was made clearer instead.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"dis\"><div class=\"who\">The state IT agency &middot; a reference document</div>\n        <div class=\"grade\"><span class=\"from\">89 B</span> <span class=\"arrow\">&rarr;</span> <span class=\"to\">100 A</span></div>\n        <p>Their file was right and the checker was wrong: a table&rsquo;s corner header was labelled with the standard&rsquo;s third scope value, which this checker did not recognise. Fixed, and a trap document added so it can never come back.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"agree\" style=\"margin-top:26px\"><strong>Software that only pretends to work can't afford to lose an argument in public.</strong> This one has &mdash; three times &mdash; and got better every time. One of the four it won on the evidence; every one of them was settled by reading the file, not by defending the tool.</p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section>\n    <div class=\"kicker\">does it actually work? &middot; the same document, measured twice</div>\n    <h2 class=\"display\">Fail. Fix. <span class=\"good\">Re-check. Pass.</span></h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">The strongest evidence is not a promise &mdash; it is the same document graded twice. A grade here is a <strong>to-do list, not a verdict</strong>: the report names every problem and the exact steps to repair it, and the re-check runs the same rules with no memory of the first attempt.</p>\n    <div class=\"pipe loopflow\">\n      <div class=\"node\"><div class=\"lg bad\">D</div><h3>First check-up: fails</h3><p>The report lists every problem found &mdash; and the exact fix-it steps for each one.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"arrow\">&rarr;</div>\n      <div class=\"node\"><div class=\"lg act\">FIX</div><h3>The author repairs it</h3><p>Headings, image descriptions, table labels, reading order &mdash; following the steps in the report.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"arrow\">&rarr;</div>\n      <div class=\"node\"><div class=\"lg good\">A</div><h3>Re-checked: passes</h3><p>Same document, same rules. The grade moved because the file did.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"statrow\" style=\"margin-top:26px\">\n      <div class=\"stat\"><div class=\"n act mono\">49</div><div class=\"l\">documents came back for a re-check after repairs &mdash; in the last 30 days alone</div></div>\n      <div class=\"stat\"><div class=\"n good mono\">9</div><div class=\"l\">of them climbed all the way to an A &mdash; failed, fixed, and passed under the same rules</div></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"agree\"><strong>This is the question behind every other question &mdash; and the answer no sales pitch can fake:</strong> the same file, failed and then passed.</p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section>\n    <div class=\"kicker\">the comparison &middot; siteimprove</div>\n    <h2 class=\"display\">Different tools, different jobs. Both viable.</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">Many agencies already have SiteImprove. Being honest about both is the fastest way to trust either.</p>\n    <div class=\"versus\">\n      <div class=\"dis\">\n        <div class=\"whoV\">SiteImprove</div>\n        <h3 class=\"pro\">What it does well</h3>\n        <ul>\n          <li>Watches an entire <strong style=\"color:var(--text)\">website</strong> &mdash; crawls every page on a schedule, tracks trends over months</li>\n          <li>Governance at scale: dashboards, policies, history for a whole organization</li>\n        </ul>\n        <h3 class=\"con\">What it isn't built for</h3>\n        <ul>\n          <li>The document in your hand right now &mdash; single-file answers wait on crawl cycles</li>\n          <li>Its scores mix the legal AA requirements with stricter AAA and best-practice items the law does not name &mdash; a sub-100 is not necessarily a legal problem</li>\n          <li>It is a paid enterprise subscription</li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"dis\">\n        <div class=\"whoV\">This checker</div>\n        <h3 class=\"pro\">What it does well</h3>\n        <ul>\n          <li>One file, answered in about a minute &mdash; <strong style=\"color:var(--text)\">free</strong>, no account, nothing stored</li>\n          <li>Scores against the legal standard, with every finding labeled by the rulebook it belongs to</li>\n          <li>Fix-by-fix instructions, plus two independent verdicts on every report</li>\n        </ul>\n        <h3 class=\"con\">What it isn't built for</h3>\n        <ul>\n          <li>It does not crawl websites, watch trends, or keep dashboards</li>\n          <li>One document at a time, on demand</li>\n          <li>Like every automated checker &mdash; SiteImprove and Acrobat included &mdash; it sees only the 30&ndash;40% a machine can judge</li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"agree\"><strong>Use SiteImprove to watch the whole site. Use this when a document is in your hand and you need an answer now.</strong> The advantage here is simple: it is faster, and it is free.</p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section>\n    <div class=\"kicker\">the evolution &middot; march to august 2026</div>\n    <h2 class=\"display\">Six months. 831 commits. 152 versions.</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">This is not a weekend project. Since March it has grown from a single PDF check into a checker for four kinds of files &mdash; <strong>391 saved changes in the last 30 days alone</strong>, its battery of self-checks grown from about 1,500 in June to 3,437 today. Every strange document an agency has thrown at it became a documented fix. All of it is public, on GitHub: <a href=\"https://github.com/ICJIA/file-accessibility-audit/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\" style=\"color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:var(--ext)\">the full change log</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/ICJIA/file-accessibility-audit/tags\" style=\"color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:var(--ext)\">every numbered version</a>, open for anyone to read.</p>\n    <!-- Newest first (user request 2026-08-28). KEEP THIS CURRENT: it is the\n       page a sceptical reader scrolls to for \"is this still maintained?\", and\n       a timeline that stops years before the version number in the heading\n       answers that badly. Add an entry whenever a release changes what a\n       reader can see or how a grade is reached; skip pure fixes and internal\n       work. build-brief warns when the newest entry falls too far behind. --><div class=\"timeline\">\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.151</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Measured its own light mode, and found it wanting</b><span>This site opens in dark mode, so every automatic check it runs on itself had only ever looked at one of the two appearances it offers &mdash; including the checks behind its own claim that both were fine. Switching the default and re-testing found 136 places where text was too faint to read, one of them at roughly a quarter of the required contrast. All fixed, and every page now passes in both appearances. Publishing the number rather than quietly correcting it is the only consistent thing to do, on a tool that checks other people&rsquo;s documents for exactly this.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.150</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>PowerPoint catches a heading that only looks like one</b><span>Text made big and bold to stand in for a heading is a real accessibility failure, and Word has always caught it. PowerPoint reported nothing at all &mdash; the one place the checker said too little rather than too much, which is the more damaging way to be wrong.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.149</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>A perfect score stopped claiming silence</b><span>A check could score 100 and still print something it had found &mdash; and label itself &ldquo;no issues found&rdquo; over the top. Those now read &ldquo;no scored issues&rdquo;: nothing counted against the document, and something was still worth saying. 128 labels changed; not one score did.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.148</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Stopped calling a real failure &ldquo;not applicable&rdquo;</b><span>A reader spotted a report showing five &ldquo;not applicable&rdquo; labels about headings, on a document whose heading structure had scored zero. That label was itself the wrong answer and was withdrawn the same afternoon: rows about something already graded are simply gone from the list now. What remains is only genuine extra credit &mdash; worth doing, required by nothing &mdash; so nothing in it can repeat a point the action plan has already made.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.147</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>An audit survives leaving the page</b><span>Opening the status page or following a link while a document was being checked used to cancel it and demand the file again. The check actually runs on the server, so the page now remembers which one is yours and picks it back up on return &mdash; and the warning about leaving appears only when leaving would really lose something.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.146</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Stopped taking points it could not tie to the law</b><span>An audit of its own test suite found nine checks that could no longer fail for the reason they were written &mdash; and three scoring rules quietly escaping the rule that every deduction must name a WCAG criterion. A Word file with two &ldquo;click here&rdquo; links had been losing an entire category at the highest severity while the verdict named nothing. Weak link text is now reported and never counted; fourteen new trap documents make sure it stays that way.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.144</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Audited its own claims &mdash; and found them wanting</b><span>Four hostile reviews, checking every statement the reports make against the standards bodies&rsquo; own pages. They found a false accusation this checker had just shipped, compliance dates the Department of Justice had moved, and five wrong citations. All corrected, and two automatic checks widened so the same classes of error stop a release.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.143</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Says what is worth doing beyond the law</b><span>Every report now lists the practices that are not required but still help readers &mdash; with this document&rsquo;s own evidence for each, and a plain statement that none of it touched the grade.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.130&ndash;136</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>The grade measures the law, and only the law</b><span>Every check in the scoring engine was re-examined against one rule: a deduction must name a failing WCAG 2.1 criterion. Fifteen checks that measured good practice instead were moved out of the score and are now reported beside it.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.117</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Passes its own trap test, 149 / 149</b><span>And caught the first of its own bugs doing it &mdash; every one since is documented above, chips and all.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.109</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Fixed the outlier that refused big reports</b><span>A 246-page annual report was wrongly called \"too complex\" &mdash; traced to the server, not the file, and fixed with proof.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.110</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Made itself stricter</b><span>Headings now judged by what they say, not just how they're numbered &mdash; scores went down, not up.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.107&ndash;111</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Loses public arguments &mdash; and fixes them</b><span>When an outside expert has been right, the file was reread, the grade corrected the same day, and the case became a trap document. Every loss is in the bug list above.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.91&ndash;95</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Covered all 31 industry checkpoints</b><span>The Matterhorn Protocol &mdash; the PDF industry's own test model &mdash; mapped on every report.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.68</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Removed all accounts and identity</b><span>No logins, no emails, nothing stored about who uses it.</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"tl\"><div class=\"ver mono\">v1.24&ndash;130</div><div class=\"rail\"><div class=\"dot\"></div></div><div class=\"body\"><b>Settled on WCAG 2.1 AA</b><span>v1.24 adopted WCAG 2.2; since v1.130 only WCAG 2.1 &mdash; the version Title II ADA and IITAA name &mdash; can move a grade. What 2.2 adds is still reported, never counted.</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </section>\n\n  <section>\n    <div class=\"kicker\">the objection</div>\n    <h2 class=\"display objection-h\">\"But... but one person built this. It's not Google or Microsoft or Adobe.\"</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\"><strong>True &mdash; and it doesn&rsquo;t need to be.</strong> This is real software, battle-tested: 12,436 check-ups for internal and external agencies, 149 trap documents designed to fool it, an independent referee co-signing every report, and a public record of every change &mdash; and it is free. Nothing here asks for trust; every claim below is checkable by anyone:</p>\n    <div class=\"checks\">\n      <div class=\"check\"><div class=\"big\">The recipe is public</div><p>Every line of code and every change is published on GitHub, for anyone to read &mdash; a kitchen that cooks with the door open.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"check\"><div class=\"big\">Independent referee</div><p>veraPDF &mdash; the PDF industry's own validator &mdash; co-signs or contradicts every report.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"check\"><div class=\"big\">3,437 self-checks</div><p>Before anything new goes live, the software re-runs every promise it has ever made &mdash; all 3,437 must pass. A single failure stops the release cold: fix it, then run the full battery again from the top. A few of those promises, in plain words:</p><ul>\n<li>A scanned page with no readable text must score zero &mdash; no partial credit for a picture of words.</li>\n<li>A file with no tables must never be graded on tables.</li>\n<li>The grade on screen and the grade in the downloaded report must match, digit for digit.</li>\n<li>An image description made of nothing but blank spaces must be caught as empty.</li>\n<li>A re-saved copy of a document must receive the identical grade, digit for digit.</li>\n<li>All 149 trap documents must still be judged correctly, every release.</li>\n</ul></div>\n      <div class=\"check\"><div class=\"big\">Public paper trail</div><p>152 version notes plus a plain-language security log written for records auditors &mdash; mistakes included.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"check\"><div class=\"big\">The best analysis tools</div><p>Inside it runs qpdf and veraPDF &mdash; the accessibility field&rsquo;s own best-of-breed analysis tools, used every day by professional remediators and certified specialists worldwide &mdash; and it checks all 31 points of the Matterhorn Protocol, the PDF industry&rsquo;s official test model. One person assembled them; the world builds and checks them.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"check\"><div class=\"big\">Free &amp; open source</div><p>No license, no subscription, no per-seat fee &mdash; run as many check-ups as you like, from any agency. And the whole thing is open source: the code can be read, copied, run on your own servers, and checked line by line. There is nothing to buy, and nothing hidden to sell.</p></div>\n    </div>\n\n    <p class=\"agree\" style=\"margin-top:30px\">To be precise about the rivals: <strong>&ldquo;Microsoft&rsquo;s checker&rdquo;</strong> is the Accessibility Checker built into Word, PowerPoint and Excel; <strong>Adobe Acrobat Pro</strong> has one too &mdash; 32 pass/fail rules. Both are respectable, and both are closed: you cannot read their code, their tests, or their history of mistakes. This page <em>is</em> that reading, for this tool. And rather than asking you to choose, <strong>every PDF report here includes an Acrobat-parity panel</strong> &mdash; the same 32 rules Acrobat runs, shown beside our verdict, so you can compare checkers without leaving the page.</p>\n    <p class=\"finalline display\">The built-in checkers ask for your trust.<br><span class=\"good\">This one hands you its evidence.</span></p>\n  </section>\n\n</div>\n";
export const TRUST_STAMP: string = "AUG 31, 2026";
